ional  family history they're often very  emotional and people talk about being  surprised completed and and everything  in between in the emotional spectrum so  I certainly had experiences like that  but I also came to see very early early  on and the research that the genetic  ancestry testing revealed the moment  when people receive the information  became a moment that allowed them entree  into other kinds of social things in the  world ways of using genetic ancestry  testing and the information that it  provides to think about other things in  the world and that's what I'm going to  talk to you about tonight so I call my  this work the social life of DNA  after the work of an anthropologist  named Arjun Appadurai who has published  a book now 30 plus years ago called the  social life of things and he suggested  to us that it was by following things in  motion right that we can illuminate  their human and social context so if I  wanted to understand following a powder  I why genetic ancestry testing was  significant not only for individual  identity and family history but beyond  these things as well perhaps I could do  this by following forms of genetic  analysis around so the social life of  DNA for me means two things it means the  way in which forms of genetic analysis  travel between sites social sites and  domains which I'll say a little bit more  about and the multiple uses to which one  type of genetic analysis is put in this  case genetic ancestry testing which I'll  also say another little bit about so we  tend to think of forms of genetic  testing and analysis in these domains  right so we typically I think  researchers regulatory bodies those of  us as individual consumers often social  scientists distinguish between medical  genetics forensic or criminal justice  genetics ancestry genealogical what we  call recreational genetics and genetic  analysis that might be used in a sort of  family court setting for family  verification or paternity what I found  in the course of talking to people about  genetic testing more generally in  genetic ancestry testing is that the way  that individuals understand genetic  testing really blurs these boundaries  right and that genetics has us so as a  social life and social power because it  has the ability to work sometimes  simultaneously in all of these domains  at once so a woman I call Sarah said to  me we think breast cancer runs in our  family  now that I understand my African  ancestry test which is a genetic  ancestry company that I'll talk about  this evening the difference between the  mother's line and the father's line and  all that I have a better sense of what  the genetic counselor at my doctor's  office was telling me Martin said to me  you know I was never really interested  in genetic science or the genome or  whatever until I heard about these  genetic genealogy tests after I took my  test I wanted to learn everything  genetics I started reading genetics  articles in the newspaper science  magazines  scientific journals and those sorts of  things so what I want to suggest here  just briefly because I want to tell you  more about the the sort of social  travels of the this company that I  studied over a decade  is in fact that one category of genetic  testing often draws Authority from its  association with other forms or domains  of testing and that individuals  experiences or attitudes about one form  of testing inform or legitimate the  broader authority of genetics so for  those of us are interested in health  disparities and in medical genetics in  particular I would want to say that for  African Americans often and for some  communities of color that genetic  ancestry testing becomes a threshold  moment the first moment where people are  thinking about genetics ever so they  might have an experience like Martin  I've never thought about genetics and  now I'm deeply interested in it or  people like Sarah who have an experience  in a clinical or medical encounter that  is informed by her interest and  experience with genetic ancestry testing  which is to say that we need to  understand the full social life right of  DNA and how people think about it in  these various domains if we want to  understand how it's important and how we  can use it with efficacy and a medical  or clinical encounter so following the  social life of DNA I came to understand  that people were using genetic ancestry  testing for these practices that I call  reconciliation projects and for me these  are sites and practices in which genetic  analysis is used to resolve  controversies or answer questions about  the past because I was working with  African Americans more particularly I  was particularly interested and a set of  a subset of reconciliation projects that  were related to perceived injuries  produced by racial slavery and the sort  of resolution of these injuries being  sought through different types of  genetic analysis so how do I go from  genetic ancestry testing identity crying  about ancestors on television to getting  here so I'm gonna go back and tell you a  little bit about how I got here and my  fieldwork this is the tombstone of a  former enslaved man and East Haddam  Connecticut named  venture Smith you  be familiar with if you want broach one  of the few slave narratives that we have  from the era of Plantation slavery his  is quite unique and quite important to  historians because he writes about his  time in the Middle Passage though he  writes about his time on the African  continent he tells us about his family  about the Middle Passage voyage he tells  us that he was on he was carried over to  the Americas on a ship that was helmed  by a captain Collingwood who was very  notorious in historical literature  because he later sort of cast enslaved  Africans off the side of another ship  and during another journey to sort of  save insurance money so we know his  whole story and so it was curious to me  in 2006 that his family gave permission  to the BBC working in collaboration with  some social scientists historians  geneticists and genetic scientists at  UConn to exhume his body and try to do  analysis on his upon his remains to find  out where he comes from so here we have  the case of someone even accounting for  the filter of the person who helped  venture Smith write his slave narrative  but we have the account his full account  in his own words of where he's coming  from and then we have this endeavor to  use genetics to find out where he's  coming from a genetics is supposed to  tell us something more  so certainly genetics could tell us a  few things that are maybe not in his  biography autobiography like what  particular haplotype groups you know he  belongs to and these sorts of things but  it still struck me as a kind of curious  endeavor so I spent some time a couple  of different occasions going to an event  called venture Smith day that happens at  the Congregational Church in East Haddam  Connecticut his family knows so much  about him and each other that they  gather annually in the fall in this town  and this in this green just next to this  church and they lay a wreath on his  grave  this is his gravesite here a member of  his family and they come from California  and other parts of Connecticut all  across the country and I asked some of  the matriarch of the family two of the  eighth generation descendants about you  know why the family permitted the sec  summation and what the information they  thought  we're gonna get so one woman said to me  I think it will help school children I  wanted to help American schoolchildren  another person explained one of his  descendants explained that she hoped it  would bring healing so I thought this  was quite poignant we're doing genetic  ancestry testing not for identity  information but because we think that  genetic ancestry testing or genetic  analysis of these remains can do  something to provide healing for our  country and for this family so this is  where I began to really think about  following genetic ancestry testing in  the various social ways that it was used  so I talked to lots of people dozens and  dozens of people over the last decade  many of these people use many different  companies some of you might be genetic  genealogist here and you probably may  view several different companies and so  you know I made a very difficult  decision to write really about one  company and the use of one companies  test for reasons I think will become  clear to you by the time I finish which  was a tough decision but but I ended up  writing about the African ancestry  company so this is the fourth genetic  ancestry company to emerge in the United  States it starts in 2003 and it's the  only first and only black owned company  so it was started co-founded by this  gentleman here an African American  geneticist named Rick Kittles who's been  at various institutions he had been at  Howard University Ohio State the  University of Illinois at Chicago and  now he's at the University of Arizona  and he's a prostate cancer researcher  and he's an entrepreneur on the side so  he starts this company in 2003 with an  african-american woman a business  partner and economist with a and the  degree in economics from Stanford named  Gina page and they are an interesting  company because you know that question  about mistrust it's in part answered and  of course embodied in the very physical  body of Rick Kittles but also because  the company went out of its way when  they were sort of advertising their  wares at these various kinds of  conventions to really say to African  Americans where the company you can  trust where the company we can trust  because we know about the experience of  these historical issues of  experimentation  where the company you can trust because  we don't keep the sample so all the  companies claim to throw out the tissue  but they actually don't keep any of the  data as well right this could be a  company worth I think a lot more money  if they were keeping all this data over  time so this company yields as an output  to its customers a certificate of  ancestry this is one for a gentleman  who's told that he's inferred to be  related to the Temne people of Sierra  Leone so this is the individual identity  piece and I found that but people I  spoke to did lots of interesting things  I mean some of them sort of got the  certificate of ancestry sort of shrugged  their shoulders and put it in a box  somewhere some people framed it and put  it in a prominent place in their home  and you know various things in between  people shared it with family members  they became sort of an object of  scrutiny and curiosity and pleasure at  family reunions so the certificate does  a lot of work although the letter that a  consumer receives will say often that  based on these you know the markers that  we are testing sort of are found in a  couple of different places right but the  preponderance of the marker we're going  to put on their certificate so there was  more complexity in that so I follow  these genetic ancestry tests of this  particular company and a set of  exercises that I call reconciliation  projects I'm not going to be able to  talk about the African burial ground  monument today but I'll just say in  brief I mean maybe we can talk about  this in QA and brief that Rick Kittles  the African American geneticist I showed  you earlier begins his career as a  junior scientist working on the African  burial ground research project in the  mid 1990s and it's here based on using  developments by sort of the geneticist  Brian Sykes who had a very important  paper in nature in the 1990s about how  to begin to work with ancient DNA  samples that were sort of decomposing  that he comes up with you know an idea  to create using samples gathered from  all over the world using them GenBank  and other sorts of sources a reference  database of african D